Multiply the short leg of the right triangle by $\sqrt{3}$ to find that the length of the longer leg is $6\sqrt{3}$ units.  Double the short leg of the right triangle to find that the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle is 12 units.  Since the hypotenuse of the right triangle is a side of the equilateral triangle, the side length of the equilateral triangle is also 12 units.  By the Pythagorean theorem, the distance between the two vertices that the two triangles do not have in common is $\sqrt{(6\sqrt{3})^2+12^2}=\sqrt{252}=6\sqrt{7}$ units. [asy]
draw((2,0)--(0,0)--(1,sqrt(3))--(2,sqrt(3))--(2,0)--(1,sqrt(3)));
draw((2,sqrt(3)-0.1)--(1.9,sqrt(3)-0.1)--(1.9,sqrt(3)));
draw((0,0)--(2,sqrt(3)));
label("$60^\circ$",(1,sqrt(3)),2SE+E);
label("$30^\circ$",(2,0),5NNW+4N);
label("6",(1.5,sqrt(3)),N);
label("$6\sqrt{3}$",(2,sqrt(3)/2),E);
label("12",(1.5,sqrt(3)/2),SW);
label("12",(1,0),S);
[/asy]

